The ACPI resource descriptors `EFI_PCI_ROOT_BRIDGE_IO_PROTOCOL::Configuration`
answered with on the one root bridge of a QEMU 11.1.0 q35 guest, under the OVMF
this repository boots every guest with — `ovmf/OVMF_CODE-pure-efi.fd`, sha256
9de33971d47958f42af86584b502f83256120b2482e4f7ed14db32fd68e92922, which `NOTICE`
also names.

  root-bridge-0.bin   186 bytes, off the `Root bridge: 0 (segment 0) 186 bytes:`
                      line the bootloader writes to the 16550 before the kernel
                      handoff, on a `Profile::Headless` boot of `tests/netcase`.

The bootloader printed the bytes; nothing decoded them on the way. How far to
print is `toyos_acpi::list_len`'s walk of the descriptor headers, and what says
that walk was right is the file itself: the four descriptors' own declared
lengths tile it exactly and the last two bytes are the End Tag, neither of which
a short or a long read would leave.

What the same boot's kernel then printed out of these bytes, which
`tests/fixtures.rs` holds them to:

  pcidev: firmware root bridge windows: mem 0xc0000000..0xc0100000, mem 0x800000000..0x800100000
  pcidev: all 9 assigned memory BAR(s) are inside them

Both memory ranges are narrower than what that machine decodes: q35 routes
everything above its RAM to PCI (QEMU 11.1.0 `info mtree` shows `pci` covering
the whole address space at priority -1, under 2 GiB of `ram-below-4g`), and the
firmware answered with the megabyte its own allocation for the bus came to. The
protocol's contract is the root bridge's *current* settings, so an answer is
always inside the platform's aperture and may be far inside it.
